{"id": "test_hand5-035", "split": "test_hand5", "intent": "no_tool", "family": -1, "request": "what's the difference between utf-8 and ascii encoding?", "expected": null, "reply": "UTF-8 (Unicode Transformation Format - 8-bit) is a character encoding that uses one to four bytes per character. It is designed to be a superset of ASCII, meaning that all ASCII characters are representable in UTF-8 with their original byte values.\n\nOn the other hand, ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) is a character set that uses 7-bit encoding. It includes 128 characters, which cover the basic Latin alphabet, digits, and some special symbols.\n\nThe main difference between UTF-8 and ASCII is that UTF-8 can represent a much wider range of characters from around the world, while ASCII is limited to a smaller set of characters used in English. Additionally, UTF-8 is designed to be more efficient than ASCII for encoding text with non-Latin character sets.", "call": null, "verdict": null, "score": {"called": false, "levels": null, "abstained_ok": true, "error": null, "answered": null}}
